# ripgrep/fd-style ignore rules (does NOT affect git; see .gitignore for that).
#
# Named `.ignore` rather than the ripgrep-only `.rgignore` so every tool built on the same
# ignore stack (ripgrep, fd, and friends) honours one list.
#
# What this actually buys, measured on this repo with warm cache: an unscoped
# `rg -l commentMode` took 5.8s WITHOUT this file and 1.1s WITH it, returning an identical
# 28-file result set. Nearly all of that is the multi-MB SINGLE-LINE minified bundles
# (dev/assets/vendor/mermaid.min.js is 3.4 MB on one line), which are pathological for a
# line-oriented regex scan.
#
# Two things this file does NOT fix, so do not attribute them to it:
#   * `.worktrees/` is already skipped via .gitignore, so listing it here changes nothing
#     for ripgrep today. It is kept as belt-and-braces for tools that read .ignore but not
#     .gitignore, and because each worktree is its own git root.
#   * A COLD first search is much slower than a warm one (12s vs ~1s was measured) because
#     Windows Defender scans each file on first read. Only a Defender exclusion for the
#     repository directory fixes that; an ignore file cannot.
#
# Keep this list to things that are NEVER a useful search target. Generated files that a human
# or agent may legitimately want to read (site/dist, the built example reports) are deliberately
# NOT listed. To search anything below anyway, pass `rg --no-ignore`.

# Parallel worktrees: duplicate copies of the whole tree (see AGENTS.md).
.worktrees/

# Dependency trees and caches.
node_modules/
__pycache__/
.venv/

# Vendored and minified bundles: single-line third-party blobs, never edited here.
*.min.js
*.min.css

# Packaged archives (binary; rg cannot search them usefully anyway).
*.zip

# Test/build scratch output.
test-results/
playwright-report/
.playwright-cli/
audit_out/
